1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:00,701
Throw!

2
00:00:00,940 --> 00:00:01,780
They're tiny.

3
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:03,800
It's gonna just rip you to shreds.

4
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:05,720
But deadly.

5
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:08,200
With a lighter weapon, that soldier can get the edge.

6
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:10,120
Robotic planes.

7
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:12,160
Mini tanks.

8
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:14,860
And top secret spy tech.

9
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:16,700
Less is definitely more.

10
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:19,180
Bigger isn't always better.

11
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:22,700
Having a lighter weapon may actually be the difference between life and death.

12
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:24,200
Tiny weapons.

13
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:27,240
Now on Modern Marvels.

14
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,380
The evolution of weapons follows two distinct trends.

15
00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:48,360
The quest to create bigger, more powerful killing machines.

16
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:52,040
And what seems like the opposite.

17
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:59,820
The design of smaller, lighter, and more mobile firearms.

18
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Throughout time, the idea is that a weapon is a compromise between range, lethality,

19
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:14,000
and accuracy.

20
00:01:14,001 --> 00:01:23,000
When a weapon is developed, it's usually heavy, it's large, it is meant to be very powerful.

21
00:01:23,001 --> 00:01:28,000
But for every class of weapon, engineers also work to transform the big and bulky

22
00:01:28,001 --> 00:01:30,860
into the lean and mean.

23
00:01:31,780 --> 00:01:35,280
And so rifles have gotten smaller.

24
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:40,040
Machine guns have gotten smaller.

25
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:44,440
And for every one of these heavy weapons,

26
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:47,040
we've had a development of a lighter weapon.

27
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:52,180
Tiny weapons can do things bigger firearms can't.

28
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:56,520
They can be concealed and carried farther and faster.

29
00:01:57,420 --> 00:02:01,180
Soldiers with small light weapons can carry more supplies and ammunition.

30
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:09,720
And with a tiny handgun, spies and criminals can get away with murder.

31
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:15,780
One tiny pistol developed in the 19th century became so notorious,

32
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:18,920
it ushered in a whole new era of smaller guns.

33
00:02:18,921 --> 00:02:31,560
In 1865, a Derringer, a six-inch pistol small enough to fit in a pocket, was used to commit

34
00:02:31,561 --> 00:02:37,232
one of the most shocking crimes in American history. We all know the story.

35
00:02:37,233 --> 00:02:38,140
A flamboyant

36
00:02:38,141 --> 00:02:43,080
actor named John Wilkes Booth snuck into President Abraham Lincoln's box at Ford's Theater.

37
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:47,120
He fired at the president's head from point-blank range.

38
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:52,080
But the reason Booth had to get so close to his target?

39
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:53,900
The Derringer.

40
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:58,060
This new tiny pistol wasn't accurate at long range.

41
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:01,280
It would spray bullets every which way out of the barrel.

42
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:04,960
Today's Derringers may look a lot like the old ones,

43
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:07,520
but they're very different inside.

44
00:03:07,521 --> 00:03:14,200
Cobra Firearms, located in Salt Lake City, produces about 1,500 tiny derringers every

45
00:03:14,201 --> 00:03:15,200
week.

46
00:03:15,201 --> 00:03:17,280
This is the beginning of a gun right here.

47
00:03:17,281 --> 00:03:18,280
This is a barrel insert.

48
00:03:18,281 --> 00:03:20,420
We're going to put that important twist.

49
00:03:20,421 --> 00:03:27,640
The twist is called rifling, and it's one of the most important advancements in gun design.

50
00:03:27,641 --> 00:03:31,560
Small spiral grooves are cut inside the barrel.

51
00:03:31,561 --> 00:03:36,940
As the bullet travels through, it rides these grooves and begins to spin.

52
00:03:36,941 --> 00:03:41,900
When the bullet leaves the barrel, the spin continues, improving aerodynamic stability

53
00:03:41,901 --> 00:03:42,900
and accuracy.

54
00:03:42,901 --> 00:03:48,300
It keeps the centrifugal force of that bullet traveling in a single direction rather than

55
00:03:48,301 --> 00:03:51,440
it wanting to tumble throughout the air.

56
00:03:51,441 --> 00:03:56,640
First introduced in rifles, the process of rifling spread to other firearms, making them

57
00:03:56,641 --> 00:04:00,760
more accurate over longer ranges.

58
00:04:00,761 --> 00:04:05,220
The Derringer that shot Lincoln did have some rifling in its barrel, but that wasn't enough

59
00:04:05,221 --> 00:04:07,100
to make the gun accurate at short range.

60
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:09,920
Early derringers also had a flaw.

61
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:12,780
The gun's barrel bores were larger than its bullets.

62
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:17,220
The pistol used to kill Lincoln had a .44 caliber gun barrel,

63
00:04:17,780 --> 00:04:20,400
but the bullet was only .41 caliber.

64
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:22,680
This mere three hundredths of an inch

65
00:04:22,681 --> 00:04:25,360
would cause the bullet to bounce around inside the barrel.

66
00:04:26,420 --> 00:04:27,500
Once it left the gun,

67
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:30,440
there was no telling which direction the bullet would fly.

68
00:04:30,441 --> 00:04:37,820
Today, every Cobra Derringer features a rifled barrel insert with a tighter fit.

69
00:04:37,821 --> 00:04:42,880
Once the rifling is complete, the barrel insert is cast into the barrel.

70
00:04:42,881 --> 00:04:44,880
Now we've got a raw Derringer barrel.

71
00:04:44,881 --> 00:04:48,400
She's going to get mated up with her frame, they're going to travel through the system

72
00:04:48,401 --> 00:04:49,400
and turn into a firearm.

73
00:04:49,401 --> 00:04:53,200
We're going to start at the all-important hinge hole.

74
00:04:53,201 --> 00:04:56,500
The barrel and frame are connected at the hinge hole.

75
00:04:56,501 --> 00:04:59,880
This allows the weapon to open and close for loading.

76
00:04:59,881 --> 00:05:02,840
Next, workers drill the lock hole.

77
00:05:02,841 --> 00:05:04,660
Once they put the lock in position,

78
00:05:04,661 --> 00:05:08,900
the barrel will remain fixed to the frame for firing.

79
00:05:08,901 --> 00:05:13,120
Derringers have been manufactured since 1852.

80
00:05:13,121 --> 00:05:15,200
With the Lincoln assassination, they

81
00:05:15,201 --> 00:05:17,580
became so popular that other manufacturers

82
00:05:17,581 --> 00:05:22,780
began copying their design, but with a very subtle difference.

83
00:05:22,781 --> 00:05:26,340
If you were to look at an actual Derringer made by Derringer,

84
00:05:26,341 --> 00:05:28,860
the spelling is with one single R. Now,

85
00:05:28,861 --> 00:05:32,100
There were attempts to copy his weapons even in the day that he made them,

86
00:05:32,360 --> 00:05:34,880
and they spelled the name with two R's.

87
00:05:35,580 --> 00:05:39,440
Henry Derringer continued making guns until his death in 1868.

88
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:43,880
But Derringer spelled with two R's has now become a generic term

89
00:05:43,881 --> 00:05:46,360
for any small one- or two-round pistol.

90
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:51,680
Another key difference between 19th century Derringers and today's

91
00:05:51,681 --> 00:05:53,700
is the emphasis on safety.

92
00:05:55,060 --> 00:05:57,620
Cobra strives to make their weapons as safe as possible.

93
00:05:58,860 --> 00:06:02,780
This cross bolt safety that we've added to it prevents what's called drop fires.

94
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:07,440
So if the firearm is ever dropped on the ground and strikes its hammer when loaded, it won't ever discharge.

95
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:12,120
28 individually machined parts go into making every Cobra Derringer.

96
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:15,660
When they're all in place, the gun is tested.

97
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:22,060
We will want to test each and every gun before it leaves Cobra, so the end user is going to be safe.

98
00:06:22,061 --> 00:06:28,120
Weighing less than a pound and loaded with .22 caliber ammunition, this virgin

99
00:06:28,121 --> 00:06:31,620
Derringer is ready for its debut.

100
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:41,760
Got him. From the start, a little Derringer appealed to a variety of gun

101
00:06:41,761 --> 00:06:47,520
toters. Gamblers could easily hide them up their sleeves and they became a

102
00:06:47,521 --> 00:06:51,120
a favorite of women looking for extra protection.

103
00:06:51,121 --> 00:06:53,620
The most common use would have been among prostitutes

104
00:06:53,621 --> 00:06:55,420
who would have needed to defend themselves

105
00:06:55,421 --> 00:06:58,960
against unwanted attacks.

106
00:06:58,961 --> 00:07:01,500
The Derringer remained popular.

107
00:07:01,501 --> 00:07:04,400
But as technology continued to improve,

108
00:07:04,401 --> 00:07:08,540
small handguns got even smaller and cheaper.

109
00:07:08,541 --> 00:07:12,080
Not surprisingly, criminals and gangsters embraced them,

110
00:07:12,081 --> 00:07:14,780
inspiring a name for their weapon of choice,

111
00:07:14,781 --> 00:07:16,840
the Saturday Night Special.

112
00:07:16,841 --> 00:07:21,660
This was the gun that you took out on Saturday night in case something happened,

113
00:07:21,820 --> 00:07:23,840
and if you had to, it was a throwaway piece.

114
00:07:24,480 --> 00:07:29,260
Saturday Night Special is a label given to an entire group of cheap small handguns.

115
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:33,160
Easy for criminals to talk before police could find them.

116
00:07:36,260 --> 00:07:38,820
With nearly a decade of crime-fighting experience,

117
00:07:39,460 --> 00:07:42,320
George Wunderlich has seen his share of Saturday Night Specials.

118
00:07:42,321 --> 00:07:48,820
The Astra 25 is one of those Saturday night specials that's gained a certain notoriety.

119
00:07:48,821 --> 00:07:51,820
It's very easily concealable. You can hide it in your hand.

120
00:07:51,821 --> 00:07:55,320
It's as thin as a wallet. This was the gun of choice.

121
00:07:55,321 --> 00:08:01,320
In the first half of the 20th century, gang violence exploded in the U.S.

122
00:08:01,321 --> 00:08:06,320
More gangsters meant more guns on the street. And the smaller, the better.

123
00:08:06,321 --> 00:08:12,320
For criminal, concealment is vitally important because it allows you to both approach and

124
00:08:12,321 --> 00:08:15,320
from a crime scene without really being able to be noticed.

125
00:08:15,321 --> 00:08:20,320
A small, concealable weapon gives you the advantage of surprise.

126
00:08:20,321 --> 00:08:24,320
Another Saturday night special, the Rome RG-14,

127
00:08:24,321 --> 00:08:27,320
nearly took the life of another commander-in-chief.

128
00:08:29,320 --> 00:08:31,320
One of the most famous uses of a Saturday night special

129
00:08:31,321 --> 00:08:34,320
would have been the attempted assassination of President Reagan.

130
00:08:34,321 --> 00:08:37,320
And what we see here is a very small, very cheap,

131
00:08:37,321 --> 00:08:40,320
.22-caliber revolver being used

132
00:08:40,321 --> 00:08:43,080
that nearly brings down the president of the United States.

133
00:08:44,160 --> 00:08:47,160
As these miniature pistols became more and more prevalent,

134
00:08:47,780 --> 00:08:49,700
the authorities stepped in to control them.

135
00:08:50,620 --> 00:08:53,800
The Saturday night special was actually outlawed

136
00:08:53,801 --> 00:08:56,280
from import or manufacture here in the United States.

137
00:08:56,840 --> 00:09:01,160
The government was compelled to get these tiny and inexpensive firearms off the streets.

138
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,000
But it would take more than a new law to stop crime.

139
00:09:06,840 --> 00:09:09,540
Tiny weapons are the go-to tool for criminals.

140
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:15,680
They can hide them in a pocket, up a sleeve, or in the palms of their hands.

141
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:22,560
Every year, tens of thousands of small handguns are used in crimes like robbery and murder.

142
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:29,240
But fortunately for the cops, and the rest of us, one small pistol helped level the playing field.

143
00:09:31,380 --> 00:09:36,180
As they used to say in the West, God did not make man equal. Colonel Colt did.

144
00:09:36,181 --> 00:09:41,580
Weapon maker Samuel Colt's pocket revolver gave police something they'd never had before,

145
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:43,900
a pistol small enough to hide.

146
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:47,380
You can now take that revolver and place it into a pocket.

147
00:09:47,620 --> 00:09:52,560
It's fully concealed, it's very handy, it's lightweight, and it has multiple shots.

148
00:09:54,220 --> 00:09:59,400
Colt's pocket revolver was the first revolver reliable and accurate enough to be adopted by police.

149
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:05,340
Its secret? Superior design and manufacturing techniques.

150
00:10:06,180 --> 00:10:09,980
It was one of the first guns in America to really use interchangeable parts

151
00:10:09,981 --> 00:10:13,740
and a high level of accurate machining to make the weapon.

152
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:19,780
The development of the Colt Pocket Revolver unleashed a variety of new, increasingly smaller pistols.

153
00:10:21,060 --> 00:10:26,280
Eventually, it ushered in a new style of policing, the hidden second firearm.

154
00:10:26,281 --> 00:10:29,760
It might be carried in an ankle holster, it might be carried in a pocket,

155
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:35,260
but typically it's concealed in a place that's easily accessible, but not obvious.

156
00:10:36,060 --> 00:10:39,960
Officers call it a backup piece, or the last line of defense.

157
00:10:40,660 --> 00:10:42,460
And it keeps the bad guys guessing.

158
00:10:43,560 --> 00:10:47,640
A criminal is going to know that somewhere on that officer's body there's a backup weapon,

159
00:10:47,820 --> 00:10:49,640
and they don't know where it is.

160
00:10:49,780 --> 00:10:52,380
Therefore, they can't take countermeasures to prevent its use.

161
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:57,100
Backup pieces are usually quite small, often less than six inches in length.

162
00:10:57,820 --> 00:11:03,020
But when an officer wants to go tiny, he or she can turn to the .22 short.

163
00:11:03,720 --> 00:11:08,460
The .22 short mini-revolver is the smallest production firearm being made today.

164
00:11:09,120 --> 00:11:13,600
This tiny gun is only four inches in length, and it weighs just four ounces.

165
00:11:14,720 --> 00:11:17,180
It can be hidden almost anywhere in the body,

166
00:11:17,300 --> 00:11:20,420
even in places that other guns you wouldn't even want to think about.

167
00:11:20,800 --> 00:11:23,020
Of course, criminals carry backup pieces, too.

168
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:27,480
Police officers must be ever vigilant for tiny concealed weapons.

169
00:11:28,760 --> 00:11:32,440
You must be absolutely thorough when searching a prisoner.

170
00:11:33,020 --> 00:11:36,760
You also have to be looking out at all times for where the hands go.

171
00:11:36,940 --> 00:11:39,660
You never know when it could wind up on a weapon.

172
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:43,740
The .22 short is the smallest weapon a policeman is likely to carry.

173
00:11:44,280 --> 00:11:48,340
But there's something else even tinier, the miniature pinfire pistol.

174
00:11:49,340 --> 00:11:53,460
Less than two inches in length, these mini guns are a favorite of collectors.

175
00:11:54,680 --> 00:11:58,680
The key to making the pinfire so tiny lies in the weapon's ammunition.

176
00:11:58,681 --> 00:12:03,680
Pin-firing ammunition, it allows the ammunition to get almost insanely small.

177
00:12:03,681 --> 00:12:06,680
We're talking about ammunition all the way down to two millimeters.

178
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:12,680
Advancements in ammunition also contributed to the trend towards ever smaller weapons.

179
00:12:12,681 --> 00:12:16,680
Once ammo changed from flint and powder muzzleloaded systems,

180
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:25,680
to smaller self-contained metallic cartridges, pistols and rifles could get smaller too.

181
00:12:25,681 --> 00:12:30,880
The miniature ammunition in the pinfire actually fires.

182
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:35,060
But this gun shoots flares and blanks rather than bullets.

183
00:12:36,060 --> 00:12:38,200
At that point, you're not talking about a firearm.

184
00:12:38,580 --> 00:12:41,240
You're really almost talking about a toy or a model.

185
00:12:43,620 --> 00:12:46,220
Pistols aren't the only weapons with miniature models.

186
00:12:47,160 --> 00:12:48,900
Even machine guns have gotten tiny.

187
00:12:50,040 --> 00:12:52,800
When SWAT teams need a weapon with devastating firepower,

188
00:12:52,801 --> 00:12:55,140
they turn to this mini machine gun.

189
00:12:55,680 --> 00:13:02,620
Developed by Belgian manufacturer FN Herstal,

190
00:13:04,300 --> 00:13:07,620
the compact P90 is under 20 inches long

191
00:13:07,621 --> 00:13:09,740
and weighs less than six pounds.

192
00:13:10,720 --> 00:13:13,000
Operators love its small ergonomic design

193
00:13:13,001 --> 00:13:15,500
and its versatility in close quarters.

194
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:19,240
During our training scenario,

195
00:13:19,480 --> 00:13:22,100
we were really working on shooting in close proximity to one another.

196
00:13:22,300 --> 00:13:24,220
A lot of times in close quarter battle,

197
00:13:24,221 --> 00:13:29,100
You're very, very close to your teammate, and you have to practice that in a real live fire scenario.

198
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:34,620
SWAT commander Ricky Thomas frequently faces life-threatening situations in tight spaces.

199
00:13:36,220 --> 00:13:38,840
P-90 is rounded. It has rounded contours.

200
00:13:39,240 --> 00:13:42,000
It doesn't get caught on your buckles, and it doesn't get caught on your gear.

201
00:13:42,260 --> 00:13:43,980
It keeps you from getting snagged and hung up.

202
00:13:44,620 --> 00:13:50,320
To create the compact P-90, engineers at FN Herstal adopted the bullpup design.

203
00:13:50,321 --> 00:14:01,238
What the bullpup does is take this same length of barrel and receiver, and by moving it back in the stock, it gives you the same efficiency and the same accuracy in a

204
00:14:01,239 --> 00:14:03,080
shorter and lighter package.

205
00:14:04,100 --> 00:14:07,880
In standard weapons, the barrel and receiver are placed in front of the trigger.

206
00:14:09,240 --> 00:14:13,320
In the bullpup, the action is moved to the rear of the weapon, behind the trigger.

207
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:19,060
This allows the barrel to sit further back in the stock, reducing the overall length of the weapon.

208
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:28,320
Unlike the compact submachine gun, which uses 9mm pistol ammo, the P90 fires a more accurate

209
00:14:28,321 --> 00:14:33,320
5.7mm round, capable of piercing body armor.

210
00:14:33,321 --> 00:14:39,320
The round that's fired out of the P90 is designed to tumble upon impact.

211
00:14:39,321 --> 00:14:43,391
Projectile enters the soft tissue, travels 1 to 2 inches.

212
00:14:43,392 --> 00:14:45,320
At that point, the front of

213
00:14:45,321 --> 00:14:48,400
the projectile starts slowing down, the rear is still going faster.

214
00:14:48,401 --> 00:14:49,320
As it's turning, you

215
00:14:49,321 --> 00:14:51,820
you get the permanent wound cavity from here to here,

216
00:14:51,821 --> 00:14:54,320
with the nose of the projectile facing the entrance wound,

217
00:14:54,321 --> 00:14:56,320
resting eight inches into the body.

218
00:14:56,321 --> 00:14:58,320
And the P90 fires fast,

219
00:14:58,321 --> 00:15:02,320
unloading 50 rounds in less than four seconds.

220
00:15:02,321 --> 00:15:04,320
They're extremely controllable rounds.

221
00:15:04,321 --> 00:15:06,320
Everything stays on target, on the suspect.

222
00:15:06,321 --> 00:15:09,320
You're not spraying and praying the area.

223
00:15:09,321 --> 00:15:12,320
Machine guns are designed to send powerful rounds

224
00:15:12,321 --> 00:15:14,320
continuously at high speed.

225
00:15:14,321 --> 00:15:17,320
The first versions, developed in the 1860s,

226
00:15:17,321 --> 00:15:21,480
were big and powerful all right, but difficult to move.

227
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:25,160
It was more of a defensive weapon because it was not mobile.

228
00:15:25,540 --> 00:15:26,820
It could not be carried forward.

229
00:15:27,280 --> 00:15:31,280
These massive death machines were placed in key positions to defend territory.

230
00:15:32,120 --> 00:15:35,460
But it didn't take long for designers to develop a smaller version.

231
00:15:36,420 --> 00:15:37,820
The Browning Automatic Rifle.

232
00:15:38,160 --> 00:15:42,720
In its original form, this weapon weighed less than 16 pounds.

233
00:15:42,721 --> 00:15:44,660
It had a 20-round magazine.

234
00:15:45,100 --> 00:15:47,060
One soldier could easily carry it forward.

235
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:48,680
and have a fully automatic weapon.

236
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:51,820
The Browning revolutionized warfighting.

237
00:15:52,160 --> 00:15:53,260
Instead of hanging back,

238
00:15:53,800 --> 00:15:55,840
the machine gun could now go on the offensive.

239
00:15:56,480 --> 00:15:59,960
You actually use it to help you take the enemy's positions.

240
00:16:00,280 --> 00:16:03,980
